// The `interview` format, v0. This file is NORMATIVE for what a LINE is: the
// Rust parser in diet/src/formats/interview.rs implements it, and does the two
// things a PEG cannot -- pair the fences, which is counting, and group lines
// into fields, which needs to know where the wrapper ends.
//
// An interview answer is the prose a forked model returns when asked what it
// decided, learned, or got stuck on this turn. It was the most-parsed format
// in the system and the largest single source of SILENT LOSS: five defects in
// one lane, every one of them content discarded or corrupted with no signal.
//
//   * `**DECISION**:` captured the bold markers as part of the value.
//   * A wrapper code fence around the whole answer made the parser see zero
//     fields.
//   * A tag-stripping step removed a leading `#` that was part of the content.
//   * A field's second and later lines were dropped: 71 of 630 archived
//     answers lost content that way.
//   * A close-cousin register (`Decision —` for `DECISION:`) matched nothing.
//
// Each was fixed by making the parser more tolerant, and every tolerance fix
// shipped its own regression. TOLERANCE IS NOT THE FIX, A GRAMMAR IS.
//
// A sixth arrived with the first version of this grammar, found by adversarial
// review: `wrapped = fence_line ~ body ~ final_fence` DISCARDED both fence
// lines and everything after the closing one. A mechanical sweep of 22,220
// generated answers found 5,428 that lost content; the same sweep over inputs
// containing no fence lines lost none. So the shape of this file changed: a
// document is a sequence of LINES, every byte of the input is inside exactly
// one of them, and accounting is structural rather than promised. What the
// parser then does with a line it must say, and `Answer::accounted_text`
// hands back everything it kept.
//
// Two rules look like tolerance and are not:
//
//   * THE TAG VOCABULARY IS CLOSED. Treating any `Word:` as a tag
//     reintroduces the continuation bug in a new dress, because ordinary
//     prose contains colons (`The bug: the parser dropped lines`) and every
//     one of them would end the field it sits in.
//   * A TAG NAME ENDS AT A WORD BOUNDARY, AND A DASH SEPARATOR NEEDS
//     WHITESPACE ON BOTH SIDES. Without the first, `## DECISIONS ABOUT THE
//     PARSER` was a DECISION field whose value began `S ABOUT THE PARSER`.
//     Without the second, `Evidence-based debugging` was an EVIDENCE field
//     reading `based debugging` -- ordinary hyphenated English fabricating
//     typed entries out of continuation lines.

// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
// document
// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------

// Every byte of the input is inside exactly one line, and every line is
// classified. Nothing here can drop anything, because nothing here chooses
// what to keep.
document = { SOI ~ line* ~ EOI }
line = { tag_line | fence_line | text_line }

// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
// tag lines
// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------

// The registers, all of them, in one place:
//
//   DECISION: value              bare
//   **DECISION**: value          emphasised, any of the markdown emphases
//   **DECISION:** value          emphasis around the separator too
//   - **DECISION**: value        as a list item
//   1. DECISION: value           as a numbered list item
//   ## DECISION                  heading, value on the lines below
//   ## DECISION: value           heading and separator together
//   Decision — value             cousin register, any of the separators
//   decision: value              any case
tag_line = { hspace* ~ bullet? ~ (heading_tag | inline_tag) ~ hspace* ~ value_head ~ line_end }

bullet = { (("-" | "*" | "+") ~ hspace+) | (ASCII_DIGIT+ ~ ("." | ")") ~ hspace+) }

// A heading with no separator must have nothing else on its line. Without
// that, `# Evidence of the leak was everywhere` was an EVIDENCE field whose
// value began `of the leak`.
heading_tag = { heading_marker ~ hspace* ~ tag_body ~ ((hspace* ~ separator) | (hspace* ~ &line_end)) }

inline_tag = { emphasised_through_separator | (tag_body ~ separator) }

tag_body = { emphasised_tag | tag_name }
emphasised_tag = { PUSH(emphasis) ~ tag_name ~ POP }
emphasised_through_separator = { PUSH(emphasis) ~ tag_name ~ separator ~ POP }
emphasis = { "***" | "**" | "__" | "*" | "_" | "`" }

// `#` opens a heading only in front of a tag name. A `#` at the head of a
// value line is content and stays there.
heading_marker = { "#"+ }

// A colon may hug the tag; a dash may not, because `Evidence-based` and
// `Plan-of-record` are words. Both must be followed by whitespace or the end
// of the line, so a separator inside a word is not a separator.
separator = { (hspace* ~ colon) | (hspace+ ~ dash ~ &(hspace | nl | EOI)) }
colon = { ":" | "：" }
dash = { "—" | "–" | "--" | "-" | "=" | "»" | ">" }

// The closed vocabulary, ending at a word boundary. Longest alternative first
// where one is a prefix of another, because PEG choice is ordered.
tag_name = @{ tag_word ~ !tag_char }
tag_word = _{
    ^"api_surface" | ^"api surface" | ^"api-surface"
  | ^"decision" | ^"evidence" | ^"learned" | ^"stuck" | ^"plan"
}
tag_char = _{ ASCII_ALPHANUMERIC | "_" }

// The remainder of the tag line: the field's first line of value, which may
// be empty when the value starts on the line below.
value_head = @{ (!nl ~ ANY)* }

// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
// fence lines
// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------

// The marker is captured separately so the parser can pair fences by marker:
// a block opened with `~~~` is not closed by one opened with a backtick run.
fence_line = { hspace* ~ fence_marker ~ fence_info ~ line_end }
fence_marker = @{ ("`"{3,}) | ("~"{3,}) }
fence_info = @{ (!nl ~ ANY)* }

// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
// text lines
// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------

// Consumes a newline, or at least one character at end of input. A rule that
// can match the empty string inside a repetition never terminates.
text_line = { line_content ~ nl | line_rest }
line_content = @{ (!nl ~ ANY)* }
line_rest = @{ (!nl ~ ANY)+ ~ &EOI }

line_end = _{ nl | &EOI }
nl = _{ "\r\n" | "\n" }
hspace = _{ " " | "\t" }
